	// text
	this._abstract = new cjs.Text("Dim Sum 點心\nThis work manifested over dim sum: in 2024, four Chinese women gathered in Boston, USA, to have lunch and share stories, practicing their Cantonese, a Chinese dialect that originates from the Guangdong province. Because Cantonese has also historically been an official language of Hong Kong, and because of the British colonization of Hong Kong from 1841 to 1997, the Chinese people who emigrated for the West—especially the Western British Commonwealth—in much of the 20th Century often spoken Cantonese. It is also owing to this fact that the most commonly found dim sum dishes in the West are still of Cantonese origin. Perhaps you have tried a few?: ha gow. Siu mai.\n\nEach of us speak, read, and write Cantonese to varying degrees of proficiency. Like food, language becomes a way for us to connect to each other in an embodied way—through a mother tongue that is less and less common, but which, like a tongue that speaks and tastes, and also cries in pain and joy, is deeply rooted to our identities as Chinese women.\n\nThe eight dishes represented in Dim Sum 點心—which literally means \"touch the heart\"—originate from each of our respective ancestral homes, including Hong Kong (“Bao Bao,” “Ha Gow and Siu Mai,” “Malay Cake,” “Wife Cake”), Foshan (“Chen Chun Fun,” “Sa Kay Ma”), Samshui (“Ham Sui Gok”), and Shanghai (“Siu Long Bao”). We let the foods inspire us toward stories in poetry and prose that represent our unique and intersecting lands, cultures, family histories, and life experiences. Along the way, it is inevitable that we address larger themes that may be faint or prominent, including of colonialization, globalization, the Second World War, and of course, starvation.\n\nWhile the web may increasingly be a contested platform of community and creativity, like the many warm layers of a fluffy Malay cake, it is also the shared and accessible space through which we can address layers of meaning in Cantonese images, texts, sounds, and tastes. That we can write in Chinese characters on the digital interface for readers, through what are otherwise American-English dominated platforms of code, we can also make shared spaces for ourselves and for others who share our languages: Cantonese. Mandarin. English. HTML5. JavaScript. Use their language; write your own stories.\n\nCapturing complex histories, uncertain present times, and hopeful futures, Dim Sum 點心 holds the flows of language and seeks to fill bellies, starting with our own.", "9px 'Hiragino Mincho Pro W3'");
