	this.text = new cjs.Text("Lai-Tze Fan was born and raised in Toronto, Canada. Her father is from Hong Kong and his family are originally from Samshui, Guangdong. Her mother is from Shanghai and was born one year before the Great Chinese Famine.\n\nSiu long bao (or xiao long bao in Mandarin) originate from Shanghai. I wrote this poem “Siu Long Bao” about the hard working and often invisible hands of women doing the care work of preparing food out of love, duty, or art. Much like the also-invisible process of making art, while preparing dumplings can be an individual task, it is also very fun to do so in collaboration with others—and this is often when the most beautiful stories emerge and connections are made. I grew up folding pork and garlic chive dumplings with my Shanghainese mother.\n\nHam sui gok originates from Samshui (literally \"Three Waters\"), Guangdong. I wrote this poem “Ham Sui Gok” about a family myth of a man in Samshui (presumably one of my ancestors) who had three sons and wanted to divide land among them equally rather than give all of his land to the eldest son. Since ham sui gok translates to \"salty water corner,\" I imagined the flow of three waters meeting in a shared space—the place of negotiation among brothers—and wondered why the water of rivers would be salty unless filled with tears. Contemplating the four brothers of my father's family who fled Hong Kong for Samshui during the Second World War and who dealt with its aftermath, this poem addresses deep generational trauma that continues to flow.", "10px 'Arial Unicode MS'");
